Completed Chapter 5B research only. No scoring was performed.

## Evidence dossier

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: 2022 Article IV Consultation—Press Release and Staff Report","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/-/media/files/publications/cr/2022/english/1mexea2022001.pdf","claim":"Mexico maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance in 2022; the IMF estimated a 3.8% of GDP deficit and 56.2% gross public-sector debt, while largely untargeted fuel subsidies absorbed the oil-revenue windfall.","locator":"PDF pp. 1-3, especially p. 1 paragraphs 2-3 and p. 3 Selected Economic Indicators","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.imf.org/-/media/files/publications/cr/2022/english/1mexea2022001.pdf|pp.1-3|neutral fiscal stance, debt and untargeted fuel subsidies","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"],"source_type":"intergovernmental surveillance report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IMF Article IV report drawing on official data, staff analysis and Executive Board review; estimates should not be treated as proof of presidential motives.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly evaluates policies and outcomes during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for federal fiscal and subsidy policy under AMLO; monetary actions were institutionally assigned to autonomous Banco de México.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF Executive Board described Mexico's macroeconomic policies and frameworks as very strong and welcomed prudent policy, while criticizing the subsidy design and recommending larger fiscal buffers."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Quarterly Report October–December 2022","publisher":"Banco de México","publication_date":"2023-03-01","url":"https://www.banxico.org.mx/publications-and-press/quarterly-reports/%7B8B635982-D4A7-0660-74C7-287C4E2BB6F8%7D.pdf","claim":"Banco de México reported that long-term inflation expectations remained anchored around historical levels in late 2022, although above target, amid pandemic, Ukraine-war and global financial shocks.","locator":"PDF pp. 2-3, lines corresponding to global conditions and inflation expectations; especially p. 3 paragraphs before 'The Board will thoroughly monitor'","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.banxico.org.mx/publications-and-press/quarterly-reports/%7B8B635982-D4A7-0660-74C7-287C4E2BB6F8%7D.pdf|pp.2-3|inflation expectations and external shocks","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.9"],"source_type":"central-bank quarterly report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary central-bank report with contemporaneous monetary-policy analysis; the bank is autonomous, limiting personal attribution to AMLO.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers October-December 2022 and full-year conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate and indirect: AMLO nominated the governor, but the rate decisions were made by the autonomous bank's governing board.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation remained substantially above the 3% target despite anchored longer-term expectations."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development","publication_date":"2022-02-21","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37/2e1de26c-en.pdf","claim":"The OECD found that Mexico remained fiscally prudent but that investment had fallen since 2019 amid contract renegotiation and policy uncertainty, especially from government efforts to strengthen PEMEX and CFE and reverse private-investment energy reforms.","locator":"PDF pp. 47-48, section 'Rebooting investment'; HTML summary sections 'Tax revenues remain low' and 'Restarting investment and boosting productivity'","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37/2e1de26c-en.pdf|pp.47-48|fiscal prudence and investment uncertainty","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"],"source_type":"intergovernmental economic survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Comprehensive OECD survey using official and comparative data; some investment weakness predated AMLO and the pandemic.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in and explicitly assesses conditions entering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for federal energy-policy direction and contract/regulatory choices; limited for inherited low productivity and investment weakness dating from 2015.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD recognized longstanding fiscal prudence, increased social spending and public investment, and potential gains from USMCA and nearshoring."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"United States Requests Consultations Under the USMCA Over Mexico's Energy Policies","publisher":"Office of the United States Trade Representative","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/july/united-states-requests-consultations-under-usmca-over-mexicos-energy-policies-0","claim":"The United States formally challenged Mexican measures that prioritized CFE generation, delayed or denied private permits, favored PEMEX under fuel rules, and in June 2022 advantaged PEMEX and CFE in access to the natural-gas network.","locator":"Paragraphs beginning 'Specifically, the United States is challenging' and 'More recently, in June 2022'","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/july/united-states-requests-consultations-under-usmca-over-mexicos-energy-policies-0|specific measures paragraphs|USMCA energy consultation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6"],"source_type":"treaty-enforcement legal statement","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative evidence that consultations and specified challenges occurred, but it states one disputing party's legal allegations rather than a completed panel finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The consultation and one challenged policy action occurred in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strong policy nexus: measures implemented by AMLO's energy administration and aligned with his declared state-energy strategy.","contrary_evidence":["Mexico framed these policies as energy sovereignty and security measures; no final USMCA panel ruling in the target year established a violation."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"El CONEVAL presenta información referente a la pobreza laboral al cuarto trimestre de 2022","publisher":"Consejo Nacional de Evaluación de la Política de Desarrollo Social","publication_date":"2023-03-01","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza_laboral_4toTrim2022.aspx","claim":"From 2021Q4 to 2022Q4 labor poverty fell from 40.3% to 38.5%, real per-capita labor income rose 4.3%, and employment increased by 1.7 million, but labor poverty remained above its pre-pandemic level.","locator":"Summary bullets and paragraphs 152-180, especially 153-164 and 172-180","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza_laboral_4toTrim2022.aspx|paras.152-180|labor poverty income and employment","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"],"source_type":"independent statutory evaluation/statistical release","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CONEVAL calculations use INEGI's nationally representative employment survey and disclose the population-series adjustment; outcomes cannot identify one policy's causal contribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 annual and quarterly comparisons.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: federal wage, transfer, labor and fiscal policies plausibly contributed, but recovery, remittances, private employment and external demand also mattered.","contrary_evidence":["The improvement did not fully erase the pandemic setback; 2022Q4 labor poverty was 38.5% versus 36.6% in 2020Q1."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"El CONEVAL presenta información referente a la pobreza laboral al cuarto trimestre de 2022","publisher":"Consejo Nacional de Evaluación de la Política de Desarrollo Social","publication_date":"2023-03-01","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza_laboral_4toTrim2022.aspx","claim":"Distribution remained unequal in 2022: rural labor poverty was 52.3% versus 34.0% urban, urban per-capita labor income was about twice rural income, and men's average labor income was 1.3 times women's.","locator":"Summary paragraphs 155 and 161-163; detailed paragraphs 174, 186 and 228-238","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza_laboral_4toTrim2022.aspx|paras.155,161-163,174,186,228-238|regional gender and formality gaps","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.8"],"source_type":"independent statutory evaluation/statistical release","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Officially mandated household/employment-based distributional indicators with clear denominators; gaps are not themselves proof of intentional favoritism.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 measurements.","ruler_attribution":"Limited for structural gaps; AMLO had policy authority over federal labor, transfer and social policies but not full control over regional labor markets or gender norms.","contrary_evidence":["Annual labor poverty declined in both rural and urban areas, and income gains reached all quintiles in the final quarter."],"lenses":["5B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico Inclusive and Sustainable Economic Growth Development Policy Loan","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-05-31","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/556411654274244589/pdf/Mexico-Inclusive-and-Sustainable-Economic-Growth-Development-Policy-Loan.pdf","claim":"The World Bank expected the 22% minimum-wage increase and expanded noncontributory pension to support poverty reduction, while warning that most recovered jobs were informal or low-paid, women's employment lagged, education cuts could deepen learning setbacks, and regulatory uncertainty constrained private investment.","locator":"PDF pp. 14 and 20-21, paragraphs 7, 16-20","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/556411654274244589/pdf/Mexico-Inclusive-and-Sustainable-Economic-Growth-Development-Policy-Loan.pdf|pp.14,20-21|wages pensions informality education and investment","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral development-policy financing appraisal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed World Bank appraisal using official and international data; several statements are contemporaneous forecasts, identified as such.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Prepared in 2022 and evaluates the 2022 budget, wage increase and recovery.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for minimum-wage, pension, budget and regulatory policies; weaker for private hiring composition and inherited regional disparities.","contrary_evidence":["Public investment was expected to support recovery, and labor income and transfers were expected to reduce poverty despite the identified risks."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Victoria Rodríguez Ceja","publisher":"Banco de México","publication_date":"2022-01-01","url":"https://www.banxico.org.mx/getting-to-know-banco-de-mexico/d/%7B21563EA0-413B-5B69-AA4D-C529AA6251E9%7D.pdf","claim":"AMLO nominated Victoria Rodríguez Ceja as Banco de México governor for 2022-2027; she had economics training, more than twenty years of public-finance experience and had served as federal deputy finance minister for expenditure since 2018.","locator":"PDF p. 1, paragraphs 1-4","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.banxico.org.mx/getting-to-know-banco-de-mexico/d/%7B21563EA0-413B-5B69-AA4D-C529AA6251E9%7D.pdf|p.1 paras.1-4|Rodriguez appointment and qualifications","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.2","5B.3"],"source_type":"official appointment biography","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary source for appointment, education and work history, but institutional self-description does not independently evaluate monetary expertise or empowerment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Her governorship began January 1, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: presidential nomination followed by Senate confirmation; operational monetary authority remained collective and autonomous.","contrary_evidence":["Her background was concentrated in budgets and public finance rather than prior central-bank monetary-policy service; the IMF nevertheless praised the bank's proactive 2022 response."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Decreto relativo a la revisión de la Cuenta de la Hacienda Pública Federal correspondiente al Ejercicio Fiscal 2022","publisher":"Diario Oficial de la Federación / Cámara de Diputados","publication_date":"2025-02-11","url":"https://sidof.segob.gob.mx/notas/docFuente/5749268","claim":"The reviewed 2022 public account recorded spending of 26.7% of GDP, debt below both the authorized amount and the 2021 GDP ratio, and 6.874 billion pesos requiring clarification or recovery at the federal-public-sector level after audit.","locator":"Operative provisions SEGUNDO through SÉPTIMO, especially paragraphs 6-25","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://sidof.segob.gob.mx/notas/docFuente/5749268|provisions 2-7|2022 spending debt and audit amounts","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.6"],"source_type":"legislative public-account review/legal record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative audited-account review; publication is retrospective, and amounts requiring clarification are not findings of criminal responsibility.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Entirely concerns fiscal year 2022, although formally reviewed in 2025.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for federal expenditure and borrowing execution; audit observations involve numerous federal and subnational entities and cannot all be personalized to AMLO.","contrary_evidence":["Debt restraint and completion of all planned audits are favorable institutional evidence; unresolved federal amounts and recommended control improvements are limiting evidence."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Índice de Riesgos de Corrupción 2023","publisher":"Instituto Mexicano para la Competitividad","publication_date":"2023-07-17","url":"https://imco.org.mx/indice-de-riesgos-de-corrupcion-2023/","claim":"Using 2022 CompraNet data, IMCO found corruption-risk scores rose in 71% of federal institutions; 38% of procurement value was directly awarded, more than 13,000 direct awards lacked a public contract document, and 45% of purchases were posted after contract commencement.","locator":"Lines/paragraphs 69-96, especially bullets under 'prácticas de riesgo'","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://imco.org.mx/indice-de-riesgos-de-corrupcion-2023/|paras.69-96|2022 federal procurement corruption risks","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7"],"source_type":"civil-society procurement-data analysis","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Transparent analysis of government procurement records by an established policy institute; risk indicators identify exposure and process weakness, not proven corruption.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Based specifically on 2022 federal procurement.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate-to-strong for administration-wide procurement rules and oversight; individual contracts were made by many separate agencies.","contrary_evidence":["The Transport Ministry recorded the lowest risk score, showing that performance was not uniformly poor across the federal administration."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Los costos del estímulo a gasolinas: recortes y deuda. Análisis anual 2022","publisher":"México Evalúa, Números de Erario","publication_date":"2023-02-14","url":"https://numerosdeerario.mexicoevalua.org/2023/02/14/los-costos-del-estimulo-a-gasolinas-recortes-y-deuda-analisis-anual-2022/","claim":"México Evalúa estimated that 2022 fuel and PEMEX support carried large fiscal and opportunity costs, disproportionately benefited higher-income fuel consumers, and coincided with PEMEX's lowest oil production since 1979 and health underspending.","locator":"Sections 'El costo adicional de apoyar a Pemex' and '¿Primero los pobres?', paragraphs 102-132","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-011","canonical_fact_key":"https://numerosdeerario.mexicoevalua.org/2023/02/14/los-costos-del-estimulo-a-gasolinas-recortes-y-deuda-analisis-anual-2022/|paras.102-132|fuel subsidy distribution and opportunity costs","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.5","5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.9"],"source_type":"independent fiscal-policy analysis","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed analysis tied to official fiscal accounts, but some tax-loss amounts are the organization's estimates and its normative conclusions are contested.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Analyzes fiscal execution for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for fuel-price, PEMEX support and expenditure-priority choices central to AMLO's economic program.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF and government agreed fuel support materially reduced near-term inflation; IMF estimates suggested the 2022 oil windfall could approximately cover the direct fuel-subsidy cost."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.5","5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Trama de corrupción en Segalmex acumula 39 investigaciones","publisher":"La Jornada","publication_date":"2022-06-03","url":"https://www.jornada.com.mx/2022/06/03/politica/008n1pol","claim":"By June 2022, authorities had received 22 complaints and opened criminal and administrative investigations concerning alleged simulated purchases, unjustified direct awards and other possible losses at Segalmex, an AMLO-created food-security agency.","locator":"Print edition p. 8; web paragraphs 56-64","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-012","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.jornada.com.mx/2022/06/03/politica/008n1pol|p.8 paras.56-64|Segalmex investigations and alleged procurement abuses","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.5","5B.7"],"source_type":"local press report based on oversight-agency statements","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous named reporting quoting SFP case counts; allegations and estimated losses were not final adjudications.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Investigations and official acknowledgment occurred during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate institutional nexus: AMLO created the agency and appointed its leadership, while direct personal participation in alleged misconduct is not established.","contrary_evidence":["The government did initiate or support multiple investigations, one case had been judicialized, and due-process outcomes remained pending."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7"]}

## Lens coverage

- **5B.1:** Wage, pension and labor-income improvements support a broad-prosperity interpretation; regressive fuel support and weak productive investment limit it.
- **5B.2:** Direct evidence covers the central-bank governor’s qualifications, nomination and formal authority. Residual gap: no equally strong 2022 evidence comparing the full economic cabinet’s competence, loyalty, and actual delegated authority.
- **5B.3:** Covered by IMF, Banco de México, OECD and the reviewed public account.
- **5B.4:** Covered by OECD investment analysis, the USMCA energy dispute and procurement competition.
- **5B.5:** Covered by procurement-risk evidence, Segalmex investigations, energy preferences and audited-account qualifications.
- **5B.6:** Covered across public investment, education and health constraints, infrastructure, regulatory certainty and energy policy.
- **5B.7:** Covered through IMF/OECD policy appraisal, fuel-subsidy targeting, procurement controls and the mixed Segalmex investigation response. Residual gap: limited internal documentation showing whether AMLO accepted expert advice or corrected a specific failed policy during 2022.
- **5B.8:** Covered with rural/urban, gender, formality and income-quintile evidence, plus subsidy-incidence analysis.
- **5B.9:** Covered by inflation, monetary tightening, fuel support, external shocks and fiscal response.
- **5B.10:** Covered using pre-pandemic comparisons, inherited low productivity, investment trends, 2022 recovery and prospective growth constraints. Because the ruler-period is only 2022, “left trajectory” remains necessarily provisional.

## Research-wave accounting

- Documents plausibly discovered: **47**
- Underlying pages, reports, PDFs or legal records opened: **23**
- Accepted source-claim units: **12**
- Rejected after inspection or as duplicative/context-only: **25**
- Access-blocked or technically unreadable: **7**
- Newly accepted evidence: **12**
- Reused evidence: **0**; the nuclear-source index was irrelevant to Chapter 5B and was not reused.
- Distinct source organizations: **10**
- Source types: **7** — intergovernmental surveillance/surveys, central-bank report, treaty/legal statement, statutory evaluation/statistics, development-finance appraisal, legislative/audit record, and civil-society/local reporting.

Rejected material included search snippets without inspectable underlying text, later-period summaries where a 2022 primary source existed, aggregate CPI material lacking personal attribution, duplicative Spanish/English IMF pages, and reports centered on 2020-2021 rather than 2022. Access blockers included 403 responses from some gob.mx pages and internal fetch failures for ASF and budget interfaces.

The required manifest could not be written because the workspace is read-only and no filesystem read/write interface was available to retrieve and preserve the complete prior ledger. The twelve `SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON` records above are the deterministic additive manifest payload; they should be appended to the parent’s cumulative manifest without replacing prior entries.

Another Chapter 5B-specific search wave would have **limited but nonzero** value. The main material gains would be narrow: internal evidence on the economic cabinet’s delegated authority, documentary proof of policy correction or rejection of expert advice, and final program-level 2022 audit dispositions. The ten lenses otherwise have multi-source coverage, so a broad additional wave would likely produce duplicates rather than materially change the dossier.
